Agreed, and done. A contained worker's abort no longer prints; its waiter gets the message:

```goose
var why: u8[>..] = [];
while !thread_wait(thread_spawn_contained(serve, attempt), why) {
    log(why);
    why.clear();
    attempt++;
}
```

- `thread_wait(id, msg) -> bool` appends the message to a growable `u8` array, as `to_bytes(a, out)` does. `thread_wait(id) -> bool` stays for callers that only need to know whether to restart.
- The message is what the abort would have printed after `goose runtime error: `, location included, e.g. `index 6 out of bounds (length 3) (server.goose:12)`.
- The first `thread_wait(id, msg)` takes the message, and the runtime frees it. Later waits still return `false` but get no message. A caller that wants it asks once; one that doesn't loses it (to keep the  memory bounded on long runs or corner-case restart loops)
- Plain workers, main, and the aborts that still end the process print as before.

The ASan failure was unrelated: a flaky `Text file busy` in the resource-paths test, fixed in #40.

@bartwe Thanks, I looked at these. The goal is to give back what a long-running app keeps after its peak, and on Windows that includes the commit charge. DiscardVirtualMemory and MEM_RESET drop the contents but leave the pages committed, so the commit charge stays at the peak. MEM_DECOMMIT is the only one that returns it. The fault handler already recommits on regrowth, so the cost is one fault per 1 MB chunk when the stack grows again. As of 2fd4d32 the decommit also stops at a per-region commit high-water mark, so repeated trims don't sweep the whole reservation.

On Linux I kept MADV_DONTNEED over MADV_FREE. MADV_FREE leaves the pages in RSS until the kernel is under memory pressure, and RSS is exactly what people check after calling trim_memory(). The call is explicit and rare, so cheaper reuse buys little. On macOS, where MADV_DONTNEED barely affects the footprint, it now uses MADV_FREE_REUSABLE.

A lazy "hint" mode (DiscardVirtualMemory / MADV_FREE) could be added later as an option if there's demand.

Additional argument for this syntax from Claude:
```goose
extern "SDL2" {
    fn SDL_Init(flags: u32) -> i32;
    fn SDL_Quit();
    fn delay(ms: u32) = "SDL_Delay";
}
extern fn cube_root(x: f64) -> f64 = "cbrt";
```
- The name stays visible. Every member starts with fn goosename(, so the column lines up and grep works.
- It reads as "defined as that symbol." An extern has no body, so the C name takes the body's place. Nothing in Goose currently uses fn ... =, so the syntax is free.
- There's precedent. Nim puts the C name at the end too: proc delay(...) {.importc: "SDL_Delay".}.
- It's still a string, so the non-identifier case works with no extra rule.
- The drawback: on a very long prototype the C name drifts to the right edge, which makes it a little harder to check against a C header. I think that's the right trade, because callers look up the Goose name far more often than anyone checks the C one.

Additional argument from me:
This seems to match the `type alias = definition;` syntax as well.

> Because it is so portable, I don't think we need compile time conditional code compilation for this, it may be sufficient to have a stdlib function that returns a system enum (and maybe some other information while we're at it)

So I don't have a personal stake in compile time conditional code compilation, and maybe `if` and `match` already eliminate dead branches when the boolean value is known at compile time (is that what you're implying with your comment perhaps?).

Having said that: remember when we joked about `const if` in the discord? I was actually wondering at the time if that couldn't unironically be a saner `#ifdef` replacement. Or alternatively a `const match` construct.

```
// If WINDOWS, LINUX and MACOS are boolean flags
const if WINDOWS {
  // ...
} else if LINUX { // `else if` chains implicitly become `else const if`
  // ...
} else if MACOS {
  // ...
}
```
```
// If WINDOWS, LINUX and MACOS are integer values
const if OS == WINDOWS {
  …
} else if OS == LINUX { // implicitly `else const if`
  …
} else if OS == MACOS {
  …
}

const match OS {
  case WINDOWS => …,
  case LINUX => …,
  case MACOS => …
}
```
```
// Using an enum that may store more information about the system
enum OperatingSystem { 
  WINDOWS { … }, 
  LINUX { … }, 
  MACOS { … }
}

const match OS {
  case WINDOWS os =>  …,
  case LINUX os => …,
  case MACOS os => …
}
```

The idea would be that this adds the requirement that the value is compile-time known, so the compiler can catch programmer mistakes. It also signals intent to other people reading the code, and in return gives the guarantee of dead branch elimination.

If you are doing a thing that makes use of entirely different APIs under Windows vs e.g. Linux, I think you're better of writing the #ifdef in C, and then binding a single portable common function to Goose.

Goose
```
extern fn DoWindowThing(i: i64);
```

C
```
void DoWindowThing(long long i) {
    #ifdef _WIN32
        Win32WinThing();
    #else
        WaylandWinThing();
    #endif 
}
```

Certainly option 1 seems ok.

Bit unfortunate that we'll need a separate one for files, etc. At the same time not sure if I want to start dealing with file handles :)

@binji ?

Thanks, these are good gaps to close. Cases 1–3 and the `defer` fix look sound to me. Case 5 has a soundness bug with a one-line fix, and case 4 loses elisions elsewhere by crowding the fact table.

## 1. Case 5 is unsound: `fits` checks the wrong side

In `NarrowStepTerm` ([bce.h:1005](https://github.com/aardappel/goose/blob/385002cfd7e54c260a5fab839092c728246b9bdf/src/bce.h#L1005)), `fits` is `NoWrap(Term { x.b, off })` with `off = x.off + d`. `NoWrap` picks the side to check from the sign of `off`, not of `d`, and returns true outright when `off == 0`. It assumes `x.b` itself lies in the narrow type, but a narrowing cast keeps the operand's i64 term: `(i + 1) as u8` has the term `{i, +1}` (`CastTerm`). So for `d < 0` the lower side goes unchecked and both relations are asserted:

```goose
import std;

fn f(xs: f64[:], i: i64) -> f64 {
    guard i >= -1 && i <= 253 else { return -1.0; }
    guard xs.len >= 255 else { return -2.0; }
    return xs[((i + 1) as u8) - 1];   // i == -1: index 255
}

fn main() {
    var a: f64[>..] = [];
    for k in 255 { a.push(k); }
    var ks: i64[>..] = [-1];
    print(f(a, ks[0]));
}
```

With this PR the check is elided at -O0 and -O2, and it reads `xs[255]` of a 255-element array (prints 0.0). On master it aborts with "index 255 out of bounds". `((i + 5) as u8) - 3` under `i >= -5 && i <= 250` fails the same way.

Fix: check the side `d` moves towards. This also covers the constant case, since `Dist(Zero(), Zero())` is 0:

```cpp
auto fits = d >= 0 ? Query(x.b, Zero(), SatSub(hi, off)) : Query(Zero(), x.b, SatSub(off, lo));
```

With that change the repro keeps its check, the five fixtures still verify at -O0, -O1 and -O2, and `--bce-lines` over test/, bench/ and samples/ is identical to this PR's. Please add the repro to `bce_narrow_steps.goose` as a `keep`.

## 2. Case 4: phi facts evict older facts

Each phi adds its implied bound against every base either flow names, after the plain meet's facts. In the repro below one phi adds 22 to 33 facts. After about six joins the table is at `MAXFACTS`, and `AddFactB` then evicts the oldest facts first, which are usually the guards the joins sit under. Most of `to` (13 bases growing to 53) is temporaries left over from earlier inlined calls.

It shows up in `stdlib/base64.goose:54` (`d[3] = symbol(s[2] & 63)` in `encode`) at -O2 in `bench/kostya/goose/src/main.goose`. Master elides both checks; this PR keeps both. It bisects to the last commit. `encode` is a `simd fn` whose loops are meant to be check-free, so this probably costs the LangArena base64 test. I did not measure that. A standalone repro, -O2, where lines 20–22 lose their elisions:

```goose
import std;

fn sym(b: u8) -> u8 {
    var d: u8 = 65;
    if b >= 26 { d = 71; }
    if b >= 52 { d = 252; }
    if b == 62 { d = 237; }
    if b == 63 { d = 240; }
    return b + d;
}

fn enc(s0: u8[:], d0: u8[:]) -> i64 {
    var s = s0;
    var d = d0;
    var t = 0;
    while s.len >= 3 && d.len >= 4 {
        t += sym(s[0] >> 2);
        t += sym(s[1] >> 2);
        t += sym(s[2] >> 2);
        t += sym(s[0] & 63);
        t += sym(s[1] & 63);
        t += sym(s[2] & 63) + d[3];
        s = s[3..];
        d = d[4..];
    }
    return t;
}

fn main() {
    var a: u8[>..] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];
    var b: u8[>..] = [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0];
    print(enc(a, b));
}
```

Possible fixes:
- Relate the phis only to bases that are still current after the join: zero, lengths and variables at the joined generations, and the phis themselves.
- Skip pairs the joined facts already imply.
- Put the implied facts at the front, so eviction takes them first.

## 3. Case 4 cost

40 variables and 1200 compare-and-swap `if`s go from 0.09s to 0.87s at `-O0 --check` (16 variables and 300 swaps: 0.03s to 0.20s). Real programs are unchanged: kostya, goose_in_goose and the samples time the same. The `fa` × `fb` pairing in `Meet` is quadratic in the implied-fact count, so narrowing `to` would help here too.

## What I checked

- Case 1: dropping the receiver's length only when its own place was bumped relies on every length change bumping that place. `guard; call(); a[k]` already relies on that, and §5.1 rejects shrinking a receiver inside its own index.
- Case 2: `HoldsLen(sub, true)` is `PointeeWriteKill`'s predicate. `DistinctTypes` compares struct definitions, so generic instantiations stay conservative.
- Case 3: `lestated` is
  - erased by every `RebindKill`, including the ones `StripKills` makes on the live flow;
  - intersected at joins;
  - added to only after `StateLeLen`'s query;
  - kept across shifts and steps only where `RecordShift` proved them.

  Every other kill scan still counts a rebind as a shrink.
- Case 4: a base created in one branch is free in the other branch's scratch copy. The only constraints on it there are axioms that every value of its variable satisfies, so the implied facts hold on both paths.
- The `defer` fix is correct.
- These all keep their checks:
  - a variable shifted by `+=` in one branch and read as the if's value;
  - an assignment past the end in an `if` branch, a `match` arm, or an `&&` operand;
  - a rebind in one branch with the bound restated in the other;
  - a rebind inside an index.
- The suite passes on this branch (Windows, MSVC; gfx, audio and ui skipped).
- `-O2 --bce-lines` over 938 files in test/, bench/ and samples/: 17463 → 17944 elided, 189 lines gain, 2 lose (the `defer` case and base64 above), no exit status changes.

## Nits

- Two reflowed paragraphs in `docs/implementation.md` leave over-long lines (the `OtherArray in KillArgStorage` one and the `IndexTypeTerm, tmplen` one).
- `IfExpr::BceWalk`'s early returns, where one branch diverges, leave an earlier `derived[this]` in place. It looks harmless, since a stale `p` has no facts in the new flow, but erasing it before those returns would be tidier.

Initial review by claude-opus-5-5:

1. **Should `goose format` rewrite comments at all?** So far the formatter only moves comments: it re-indents them, aligns trailing ones, and keeps block comments with their line. `--verify` checks that the output has "the same tokens and comments". This PR makes it rewrite comment text, collapsing a three-line `/*`, text, `*/` into `/* text */`, and it normalizes the comments in `LexForCheck` so the check accepts that, which also means the check no longer catches other damage to such comments. That is a style opinion imposed on every user. Other formatters generally leave it off by default (gofmt doesn't do it, and rustfmt's `normalize_comments` is opt-in). There is no motivation in the PR description, and the repo's own `.goose` files contain no comment of this shape (about 10 block comments in total), so the formatter's own corpus doesn't need it. Unless there's a reason I'm missing, this is cost without a clear benefit.

2. **Width.** The 100-column check measures only up to the end of the comment, not the code that follows `*/` on the same line. This input:

   ```goose
   fn main() {
       /*
       <85 x's>
       */ let something_long = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4;
   }
   ```

   formats to one 131-column line.

Thanks, the hazard is real: locals can shadow, and inner functions can capture names. But a trial re-analysis of every root on each rename is a lot of machinery for this, and it costs more than it buys:

- Rename stops working on any program that has an error, because `valid()` requires zero errors. Rename gets used mid-edit a lot, and the test has to close the `recovery`/`broken` fixtures to keep passing.
- The existing `rename 8:4` test (a function in a file shared by two entry files) now gets refused as an "ambiguous exemplar overload". That's a false positive from comparing clones, not a real hazard.
- Any file on disk that differs from the analyzed copy also makes it refuse.
- Every rename now does a full, non-cancellable parse and check of each affected root, stdlib included.
- The `Origin()` changes to `ResolvedCall`/`PatternVariantOf`, the offset shifting and the per-clone binding comparison only exist to support the comparison, and each is more code that can go wrong.

Could you try a name-collision check on the existing index instead? It does no re-analysis: refuse when the new name already appears where the rename could interact with it. Roughly (`target.kind` and `LineOf` are stand-ins):

```cpp
// A new name that is already in use where the rename lands could capture,
// or be shadowed by, that use. A local can only interact within the file
// declaring it; anything else can also meet a declaration of the name
// elsewhere, such as an overload a call would now dispatch to.
auto local = target.kind == Occurrence::LOCAL || target.kind == Occurrence::PARAM ||
             target.kind == Occurrence::BINDER;
for (auto &root : ws.roots) {
    if (!root->analysis) continue;
    for (auto &f : root->analysis->files) {
        bool edited = edits.count(f.uri) > 0;
        if (local && f.uri != tf.uri) continue;
        for (auto &o : f.occs)
            if (LeafOf(o.name) == newname && (edited || o.IsDeclaration())) {
                error = cat("'", newname, "' is already used at ", f.uri, ":", LineOf(f, o.start),
                            "; renaming could change what it refers to");
                return false;
            }
    }
}
```

That catches both cases in your test (`first`→`second`, and `capture_name` in the unchecked inner function), keeps working on programs with errors, and leaves the existing exemplar rename alone. Duplicate globals and types are already compile errors, so they fail loudly anyway. It is conservative: renaming a local to a name used elsewhere in the same file gets refused. If that turns out to be annoying in practice, it can be narrowed to the innermost block or function span that covers the edits.

The UTF-16-correct `apply()` in `test/lsp_protocol.py` and the emoji line in the fixture are good to keep either way.

Initial review by claude-opus-5-5:

The bug is real and the fix is sound, but most of its ~240 lines can go: there's a simpler version that gets the same verdicts. I measured what each part of the PR contributes by putting it behind its own switch:

- **Snapshot (`bodyevents`):** this is the core of the fix, but on its own it is *unsound*. `put(r1, Row { a[..] }); put(r2, Row { b[..] }); a.clear();` in one caller gets accepted, where master rejects it. The caller's event for `r1` names `put`'s local `x`, and the second call remaps `x`.
- **Copy flattening (Tarjan, `calleelocals`, `madeholders`/`madecopies`, the second loop for copies into a parent's variable) and the binding remap:** these exist to close that hole. The binding remap without the flattening is also unsound.
- **`heldparam` and `storedinto`:** precision for `fn pass(x: Row) -> Row { return x; }`.

**Suggested replacement for the flattening and the binding remap.** Keep the snapshot, `heldparam` and `storedinto`, and replace the rest with one rule: a caller's event never names a holder of the callee's own.

- `source()` drops such a holder (`callerside`), so that store falls back on its bound.
- A class event that stored a copy of one instead stores what that holder holds at this call:
  - a by-value holder parameter that nothing else was stored into holds the argument's contents;
  - any other holder holds what the body stored there, read from `bodyevents` and followed through the holders it was copied from.
- That one lookup (`holds`) has a memo, deduplication, and cycle handling: a copy back into a holder still being followed adds nothing.

The diff to `typecheck_builtins.h` is below. `ast.h`, `typecheck.h`, `typecheck_calls.h`, `typecheck_flow.h` and the tests are unchanged from this PR. Overall the `src` change goes from 244 to 175 added lines (`typecheck_builtins.h` from 199 to 137), and it applies cleanly to current master.

**Measured** (Windows Release, against this PR rebased onto master):

| | master | this PR | simpler version |
|---|---|---|---|
| your 7 new tests | 3 wrong | pass | pass |
| unsound accepts, 2,500 fuzzed programs\* | 25+ | 0 | 0 |
| false rejections, seeds 5 / 6 / 11 (500 each) | 346 over seeds 5+6 | 23 / 21 / 27 | 23 / 21 / 27, the same programs |
| `goose check` over test/, samples/, bench/ (2,931 files) | | no verdict changes | no verdict changes |
| `run_tests.py --profile baseline --cc native` | | | all pass |

\* My generator, modelled on yours: direct, `put`, a copying helper, a two-level relay, `pass`, two parameters, a caller-local holder, a loop around the call, a block through a generic, a nested `add`, a copy cycle, a generic `put_g<T>`, a callee-local array, and `mk(x) { var t = x; return t; }`. Each program has 1–3 callers in random order, sometimes two imports in one caller, and clears the viewed array, an unrelated one, or nothing.

The remaining false rejections are all `mk`/`putmk`: a holder parameter copied into a local and returned. Both versions reject those. Stress shapes (an 800-function copy chain, a 1,000-deep chain with 5 callers, 2,000 local copies, a 120-field holder copied 120×, a diamond-shaped graph of copies) all check in 0.03–0.18s.

