Following up after reviewing this together with #310. Yes, it only serves large fixed values: its one user is `MovableArg` (and, in #310, `ConsumedParam`). On master alone it only changes calls passing structs over 4096 bytes, so it shouldn't land on its own. The question is what it buys #310.

**Measured: very little, and a much smaller rule gets the same code.** I couldn't build goose_imgui here, so I wrote a stand-in for its record pass (a ~520-byte `Desc` with the same nested layout, built by `var d` setter chains from `row()`/`column()`, handed to an `open_box(id, d)` that passes it on to `add_node(kind, id, var d)`, a read-only `interaction_for(nid, d)`, `box<F>`, `button`, `checkbox`, `slider`, `progress`, 1,000 rows = 15,000 nodes a frame; source in #310's comment). I added env-var switches to the PR's compiler for each mechanism and A/B'd the generated C and the frame time (WSL, Ryzen 9950X3D, pinned, best of 9-11 interleaved runs):

| | gcc 13 -O2 | gcc 13 + `-mstringop-strategy=libcall` | clang 20 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| full #310 | 1.097 ms | 0.658 ms | 0.371 ms |
| #310 without liveness (`MovableArg`, `ConsumedParam` off) | 1.162 | 0.666 | 0.381 |

Without liveness, the generated C differs in one place: `open_box`'s copy of `d` for `add_node`. That is the pattern that pays here: a parameter, or an inlined call's bound argument, handed straight on at its **only** use.

A rule that only finds those uses is about 30 lines: an Ident is a last use when it is the only Ident naming its variable in the function, it is outside any loop the variable isn't declared in, and it isn't in a deferred body or a nested function. With that rule in place of `Liveness::last` (in both `MovableArg` and `ConsumedParam`), the bench's generated C is **byte-identical** to the full PR's at -O0, -O1 and -O2:

```cpp
// The Idents that are the only one naming their variable in the function,
// outside any loop the variable is not declared in, and outside deferred
// bodies and nested functions: nothing reads that variable after them.
inline const set<const Ident *> &CodeGen::SingleUses(FnSpec *sp) {
    auto it = singleuses.find(sp);
    if (it != singleuses.end()) return it->second;
    struct U { int n = 0; const Ident *id = nullptr; int useloop = 0; int declloop = 0; };
    unordered_map<const VarDef *, U> u;
    int loops = 0;
    function<void(Node *, int, bool)> walk = [&](Node *n, int loop, bool hidden) {
        if (!n) return;
        if (auto id = Is<Ident>(n); id && id->vdef) {
            auto &e = u[id->vdef];
            e.n += hidden ? 2 : 1;
            e.id = id;
            e.useloop = loop;
        }
        if (auto vd = Is<VarDecl>(n)) for (auto v : vd->defs) u[v].declloop = loop;
        if (auto m = Is<MatchExpr>(n))
            for (auto &a : m->arms) if (a.binder) u[a.binder].declloop = loop;
        auto inner = loop;
        if (Is<While>(n) || Is<LoopExpr>(n) || Is<ForLoop>(n)) inner = ++loops;
        if (auto f = Is<ForLoop>(n)) {
            if (f->vdef) u[f->vdef].declloop = inner;
            if (f->idxdef) u[f->idxdef].declloop = inner;
        }
        auto h = hidden || Is<Defer>(n) || Is<FunVal>(n) || Is<FnDecl>(n);
        RunChildren(n, [&](Node *c) { walk(c, inner, h); });
    };
    walk(sp->body, 0, false);
    auto &out = singleuses[sp];
    for (auto &[v, e] : u)
        if (e.n == 1 && e.useloop == e.declloop) out.insert(e.id);
    return out;
}
```

A single Ident makes most of `liveness.h`'s special cases moot: there is no other use to order against (the `Alone`/full-expression rule, unordered children), no reference to the variable can exist without naming it, and an Ident reached twice in a shared subtree counts as two. With this rule (`MovableArg` and `ConsumedParam` both using it) #310's suite passes on Windows, including every program's output. The one failure is `last_use_moves`' own C check, which expects hand-overs on branches, in a `match` arm and `x = f(x)` that this rule doesn't find. (The gfx/ui/physics/audio package tests couldn't fetch their submodules here, with the full PR too, so those didn't run.)

What the rule gives up is a last use after earlier reads, on one branch, in a `match` arm or `block` break, and `x = f(x)`. How often does that matter? I compiled every program under `test/` and `bench/` that builds on its own (852) at the default -O2 with #310, with the single-use rule, and with moves off entirely. #310 changes the C of 22 of them. The single-use rule changes the C of exactly one, `fixed_pointer_abi.goose` (#310's own test), and so does turning moves off. Before taking on 473 lines for those cases, I'd want a measured program where they pay. Two more costs of `liveness.h` beyond its size:

- Its soundness rests on codegen evaluating operands in `RunChildren` order (the header says so). Nothing enforces that coupling. If a later codegen change reorders an operand, the result is a callee writing a variable the caller still reads: wrong values, not a compile error or a failing assert.
- It is a second dataflow framework next to the optimizer's and BCE's. A future user of "last use" would probably want it on the optimizer's side, before inlining, not in codegen.

So my suggestion: drop this PR, and have #310 use the single-use rule above in `MovableArg`/`ConsumedParam`. If a real case for the full analysis turns up later, it can come back with that case as its benchmark.

The pointer convention is a good idea and the speedup is real, but most of it comes from a few of the mechanisms here, and two of the larger ones can go or shrink a lot. Measurements and a suggested cut below.

**Setup.** I couldn't build goose_imgui itself here, so I wrote a stand-in for its record pass (source at the end): a ~520-byte `Desc` with the same nested layout (`LayoutDesc` with two `Size[..8]`, `SizeDesc`, `LookDesc`, `MotionDesc`, `bits`), built by `var d` setter chains from `row()`/`column()`, handed to `open_box(id, d)` → `add_node(kind, id, var d)` (which changes its copy and pushes it into a node array), to a read-only `interaction_for(nid, d)`, and through `box<F>`, `button`, `checkbox`, `slider`, `progress`. 1,000 rows, 15,000 nodes a frame, like `bench.goose`'s rows 1000. I added an env-var switch to the PR's compiler for each mechanism and timed the record pass (WSL Ubuntu, Ryzen 9 9950X3D, pinned to one core, best of 9-11 runs, variants interleaved; every variant prints the same checksum):

| ms a frame | gcc 13 -O2 | gcc 13 + `-mstringop-strategy=libcall` | clang 20 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| master | 2.165 | 1.294 | 0.417 |
| `PtrAbi` convention only (everything below off) | 1.924 | 1.112 | 0.404 |
| full PR | **1.097** | **0.658** | **0.371** |
| PR without `OpenIbFixed` | 1.432 | 0.854 | 0.387 |
| PR without `DiesAtCall` | 1.281 | 0.774 | 0.383 |
| PR without the in-place struct literal | 1.325 | 0.753 | 0.375 |
| PR without lending (`ParamUnwritten`/`StableArg`) | 1.122 | 0.671 | 0.398 |
| PR without liveness moves (`MovableArg`/`ConsumedParam`) | 1.162 | 0.666 | 0.381 |
| PR without `AliasParam` | 1.102 | 0.656 | (same C as PR) |
| PR, single-use rule instead of liveness, no `AliasParam` | 1.105 | 0.651 | 0.372 |

The last row's C is byte-identical to the full PR's at -O2. The single-use rule alone, with `AliasParam` left on, gives byte-identical C at -O0, -O1 and -O2.

**What carries the speedup:** binding an inlined body's named result at a fresh destination (`OpenIbFixed`), handing over arguments that die at the call (`DiesAtCall`), building literals in place, and lending read-only parameters (7% under clang). With the `PtrAbi` convention and `Dst::fresh`, these are about 300 of the ~930 compiler lines across both PRs, and each one's safety argument is local.

**What doesn't, at the default -O2:**
- **Liveness (#309, 473 lines plus `MovableArg`/`LivenessOf`).** It changes one copy here: `open_box` handing `d` to `add_node`. The pattern that pays is a value handed straight on at its *only* use. A ~30-line "only Ident of its variable, outside any loop it isn't declared in" rule gets byte-identical C; details and code on #309. I'd keep `ConsumedParam` on top of that rule and drop `liveness.h`.
- **`AliasParam`** (with the `reuse` path in `EmitSpecCall` and the `gs_r0 != &x` return, ~80 lines). At -O2 the setters are inlined, `OpenIbFixed` does the same job, and turning `AliasParam` off doesn't change the generated C at all. It only matters when setters stay calls. At goose `-O0`, with gcc + libcall, the PR runs 0.664 ms and 0.828 ms without it; with clang, 0.365 and 0.379. So it buys debug-build speed for builder chains. That's worth having only if -O0 speed of this idiom is a goal, and then better as its own small PR. Across the 852 `test/`/`bench/` programs that compile on their own, turning it off changes the -O2 C of one file, `export.goose`.

**The C compiler matters as much as the PR.** With clang (what goose picks on Windows, as clang-cl, and on macOS), the whole PR is -11% on this bench (clang 20 in the table; on Windows, clang-cl 22 gave a similar few percent on an earlier version of this bench). clang already removes most of these copies on master: its binary has 65 memcpy call sites, against 152 for gcc with libcall. The 2x is gcc's. On top of that, gcc 13 `-O2 -mtune=generic` here copies these structs with `rep movsq` (98 sites in master's binary, 20 in the PR's), and `-mstringop-strategy=libcall` alone takes master from 2.17 to 1.29 ms on this Zen 5. The description says your gcc calls memcpy above 256 bytes, so the libcall column is probably the one that matches your setup. None of this argues against the PR, since gcc is Linux's default `cc`. But the description should say which C compiler the -9..-18% was measured with.

**Smaller things:**
- `docs/design/large_fixed_structs.md` is cited in `codegen.h`, `fixed_pointer_abi.goose` and the first commit's message (which also refers to its `fixed_chain` benchmark), but it isn't in either PR or on master.
- `stableptrarg` and `callargs` are member state that `EmitArg` sets and `EmitSpecCall`/`EmitDispatch` save, reset and restore around nested calls. `EmitArg` returning "this argument is the caller's own storage" (or filling a `vector<bool>`) would make the result-destination rule in `EmitSpecCall` easier to check. As it stands, `EmitDispatch` sets the flag through `EmitArg` and never reads it. That's safe today: dispatch passes `later`, so it never lends, and `MovableArg` refuses callees that build results in place. But nothing says so.

**Suggested shape:** keep the convention, `Dst::fresh`, `DiesAtCall`, the in-place literal, `OpenIbFixed`, lending, and `ConsumedParam` with the single-use rule. Drop `liveness.h` (#309) and `AliasParam` (or move it to its own PR with an -O0 case). On this benchmark that keeps all of the -O2 gain with about 400 of the ~930 compiler lines, and none of the remaining pieces depends on codegen's operand order.

