The problem is real: a worker writes its `gs_stks[i]` descriptor on every data-stack allocation, and those blocks are small enough for the allocator to pack several workers' blocks into one line. Three things before this can go in:

**1. In-process runs crash on Linux (and presumably on every other non-Windows target).** The JIT compiles `runtime.h` with TinyCC against the C surface in `src/runtime/jit_c.h` (embedded through `runtime_inline.h`, not the system headers). That surface declares `calloc` and `free` but not `aligned_alloc`, so TinyCC takes the implicit `int` return and truncates the pointer. `gs_stack_block` then memsets an address like `0x55baf1c0`, and every `goose file.goose` / `goose run` segfaults at startup. Reproduced in WSL Ubuntu with a clang build: master runs `samples/01_tour.goose` in-process, and this branch exits with 139 on that fault inside memset. Adding `void *aligned_alloc(size_t, size_t);` to `jit_c.h` and regenerating `runtime_inline.h` makes it run again. CI hasn't run on this PR yet, which is why it didn't show up. Windows is fine: the samples pass both AOT and in-process.

**2. Consider padding instead of aligned allocation.** All the platform-specific parts exist only to get aligned memory: the `_WIN32` split, `<malloc.h>` in the middle of the runtime, the TinyCC prototypes, the `jit_runtime.c` exports, and the missing `jit_c.h` declaration above. A chunk doesn't need to be aligned to have its cache lines to itself. It only needs a line's worth of distance from its neighbours:

```c
/* A line of padding either side: no byte of the chunk shares a cache line
   with anything else the allocator hands out. */
static void *gs_private_calloc(size_t n) {
    uint8_t *p = (uint8_t *)calloc(1, n + 128);
    return p ? p + 64 : NULL;
}
static void gs_private_free(void *p) {
    if (p) free((uint8_t *)p - 64);
}
```

It has the same effect in portable C99, works unchanged under TinyCC on every platform, and needs no new JIT symbols. It costs 128 bytes per worker chunk.

