The `thread_local` benchmark was added to demonstrate one of the design features of Goose: fast access to thread-local data. Below are the comments from the `thread_local.goose` test:

// From goose_spec.md 11.2
//
// A thread program is an instance of the program with globals of its own.
// At thread_spawn, every global the worker’s program uses is copied from
// the spawning instance, in the same way as the arguments, and the worker
// reads and writes its copies from then on. No global is shared: a worker
// that increments a global increments its own copy.
//
// Because each instance owns its globals as plain data, reading or writing
// one is a direct load or store — no TLS lookup, no lazy-init check — which
// is why they beat thread locals in languages such as C++ or Rust, where
// an access may pay for TLS resolution or initialization state.

Thread-local variables are widely used in real-world applications and libraries. For instance, in the Rust standard library, see the `thread_local!` macro and the `#[thread_local]` attribute. If Goose offers advantages in terms of performance or convenience when working with such data, we should reflect this in our benchmarks.

> Separately, the allocator now uses 128-byte alignment rather than the portable padding suggested in the review. If you prefer to keep the platform-specific allocation paths, could you explain what they buy over padding here?

By aligning to cache-line boundaries, we reduce the number of cache lines used to access the `gs_stks` array. Since this array is used extensively throughout the generated code, this results in better utilization of the L1 cache.

